## Intervals on a line

Back on a single number axis. A segment from $a_{1}$ to $a_{2}$ is the interval $[a_{1}, a_{2}]$. Two intervals $[a_{1}, a_{2}]$ and $[b_{1}, b_{2}]$ overlap unless one ends before the other begins, so they overlap exactly when

$$
a_{1} \le b_{2} \quad \text{and} \quad b_{1} \le a_{2}
$$

<CodeGroup>

```ts
function overlap(a1: number, a2: number, b1: number, b2: number): boolean {
	return a1 <= b2 && b1 <= a2;
}
```

```js
function overlap(a1, a2, b1, b2) {
	return a1 <= b2 && b1 <= a2;
}
```

```py
def overlap(a1: float, a2: float, b1: float, b2: float) -> bool:
    return a1 <= b2 and b1 <= a2
```

</CodeGroup>

<Complexity items={[{'name': 'overlap', 'time': 'O(1)', 'space': 'O(1)'}]} />

## Rectangle vs rectangle

A rectangle whose sides run along the axes is called an **axis-aligned bounding box**, or AABB. With its corner at $(x, y)$, width $w$ and height $h$, it's nothing more than two intervals: $[x, x + w]$ on the $x$-axis and $[y, y + h]$ on the $y$-axis.

Two AABBs collide when their intervals overlap on the $x$-axis **and** on the $y$-axis. If there's a gap on either axis, you can slide a straight line between them.

<RectRect />

<CodeGroup>

```ts
type Rect = { x: number; y: number; w: number; h: number };

function rectsCollide(a: Rect, b: Rect): boolean {
	return (
		overlap(a.x, a.x + a.w, b.x, b.x + b.w) &&
		overlap(a.y, a.y + a.h, b.y, b.y + b.h)
	);
}
```

```js
// A rectangle is an object like { x: 20, y: 40, w: 120, h: 80 }.
function rectsCollide(a, b) {
	return (
		overlap(a.x, a.x + a.w, b.x, b.x + b.w) &&
		overlap(a.y, a.y + a.h, b.y, b.y + b.h)
	);
}
```

```py
@dataclass
class Rect:
    x: float
    y: float
    w: float
    h: float


def rects_collide(a: Rect, b: Rect) -> bool:
    return (
        overlap(a.x, a.x + a.w, b.x, b.x + b.w)
        and overlap(a.y, a.y + a.h, b.y, b.y + b.h)
    )
```

</CodeGroup>

<Complexity items={[{'name': 'rectsCollide', 'time': 'O(1)', 'space': 'O(1)'}]} />

AABBs are so cheap that games often wrap complicated shapes in one, and only run the exact test when the boxes overlap.

## Circle vs rectangle

Find the point $Q$ of the rectangle that is closest to the circle's centre $C$, and it's a point-in-circle test again. Finding $Q$ takes no geometry at all: clamp each coordinate of $C$ into the rectangle's interval on that axis.

$$
Q = \big(\operatorname{clamp}(x_{C},\, x,\, x + w),\ \operatorname{clamp}(y_{C},\, y,\, y + h)\big)
$$

When the centre is inside the rectangle, $Q$ is the centre itself, the distance is $0$, and the test rightly says they collide.

<CircleRect />

<CodeGroup>

```ts
function clamp(value: number, min: number, max: number): number {
	return Math.max(min, Math.min(max, value));
}

function circleRectCollide(c: Circle, rect: Rect): boolean {
	const closest = {
		x: clamp(c.x, rect.x, rect.x + rect.w),
		y: clamp(c.y, rect.y, rect.y + rect.h)
	};
	return pointInCircle(closest, c);
}
```

```js
function clamp(value, min, max) {
	return Math.max(min, Math.min(max, value));
}

function circleRectCollide(c, rect) {
	const closest = {
		x: clamp(c.x, rect.x, rect.x + rect.w),
		y: clamp(c.y, rect.y, rect.y + rect.h)
	};
	return pointInCircle(closest, c);
}
```

```py
def clamp(value: float, low: float, high: float) -> float:
    return max(low, min(high, value))


def circle_rect_collide(c: Circle, rect: Rect) -> bool:
    closest = Point(
        clamp(c.x, rect.x, rect.x + rect.w),
        clamp(c.y, rect.y, rect.y + rect.h),
    )
    return point_in_circle(closest, c)
```

</CodeGroup>

<Complexity items={[{'name': 'clamp', 'time': 'O(1)', 'space': 'O(1)'}, {'name': 'circleRectCollide', 'time': 'O(1)', 'space': 'O(1)'}]} />
